WEBVTT

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:03.386
Last video, our ratio points traced this curve,

00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:06.051
y equals the natural log of x.

00:00:06.794 --> 00:00:09.191
It answers questions that start with how long.

00:00:10.115 --> 00:00:12.339
A new app has a thousand users, and

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.364
each user brings in one new user a

00:00:14.425 --> 00:00:14.645
week.

00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:17.618
So after x weeks, it has e to

00:00:17.659 --> 00:00:19.124
the x thousand users.

00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:21.631
Turn that around, and the time to reach

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:24.197
x thousand users is the natural log of

00:00:24.378 --> 00:00:24.798
x weeks.

00:00:25.702 --> 00:00:26.727
Here's a tempting guess.

00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:29.214
E to the x is its own derivative,

00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:31.820
and the natural log undoes e to the

00:00:32.001 --> 00:00:32.121
x.

00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:34.889
So is the natural log its own derivative

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.170
too?

00:00:36.095 --> 00:00:37.701
Look at x equals a half.

00:00:38.284 --> 00:00:40.674
The curve is below the axis there, so

00:00:40.735 --> 00:00:42.603
the natural log of a half is negative.

00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:44.662
But the curve is still climbing.

00:00:45.288 --> 00:00:48.174
A climbing curve has a positive gradient, so

00:00:48.296 --> 00:00:49.077
the guess fails.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:51.973
Then what is the gradient of this curve,

00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:52.617
and why?

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:55.920
Measure it, starting at x equals one, where

00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:57.166
the curve crosses the axis.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.010
Slide a second point in from the right,

00:01:00.391 --> 00:01:02.091
and the secant's slope settles:

00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:05.733
point eight one, point nine five, point nine

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:06.294
nine five.

00:01:06.976 --> 00:01:08.570
The tangent's slope is one.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:10.244
Now move along.

00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:12.755
At x equals two, the gradient is a

00:01:12.856 --> 00:01:13.156
half.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.516
At x equals four, it's a quarter.

00:01:16.081 --> 00:01:18.007
And back at x equals a half, the

00:01:18.028 --> 00:01:18.991
gradient is two.

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:21.841
Double x, and the slope halves.

00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:24.817
Multiply the gradient by x, and you get

00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:26.124
one, every time.

00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:27.915
Let's mark that pattern.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.271
At each x, put a dot at the

00:01:30.393 --> 00:01:31.379
height of the gradient.

00:01:31.943 --> 00:01:34.070
Near zero, the curve is steep, so the

00:01:34.111 --> 00:01:34.712
dots are high.

00:01:35.336 --> 00:01:37.584
Further out, it flattens, and the dots sink.

00:01:38.327 --> 00:01:41.349
They all land on one curve, y equals

00:01:41.531 --> 00:01:42.390
one over x.

00:01:43.193 --> 00:01:45.180
But why such a simple gradient?

00:01:45.865 --> 00:01:47.680
The answer is hiding in e to the

00:01:47.842 --> 00:01:47.962
x.

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:50.785
In Year 11, you saw that the natural

00:01:50.845 --> 00:01:52.929
log curve is the reflection of e to

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:55.133
the x in the line y equals x.

00:01:55.775 --> 00:01:58.303
Reflecting in that line swaps each point's x

00:01:58.384 --> 00:01:58.785
and y.

00:01:59.368 --> 00:02:01.097
The point zero, one on e to the

00:02:01.258 --> 00:02:03.108
x lands on one, zero.

00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:05.236
On e to the x, the point with

00:02:05.296 --> 00:02:07.685
height two sits at x equals the natural

00:02:07.745 --> 00:02:09.612
log of two, about point six nine.

00:02:10.315 --> 00:02:12.500
After the reflection, it sits at x equals

00:02:12.622 --> 00:02:14.466
two, with height point six nine.

00:02:15.229 --> 00:02:17.335
Every point swaps, and together they make the

00:02:17.396 --> 00:02:18.319
natural log curve.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.562
Now carry a tangent through.

00:02:21.328 --> 00:02:23.251
On e to the x at height two,

00:02:23.613 --> 00:02:24.534
the slope is two,

00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:26.799
because the gradient equals the height.

00:02:27.541 --> 00:02:29.005
Draw its tangent triangle:

00:02:29.648 --> 00:02:31.051
across one, up two.

00:02:32.136 --> 00:02:33.863
Reflect it in y equals x.

00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:36.774
The across leg stands up, so now it's

00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:37.456
a rise of one.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:40.432
The up leg lies down, so now it's

00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:41.076
a run of two.

00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:44.676
Rise one over run two: the slope is

00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:45.078
a half.

00:02:46.162 --> 00:02:48.603
A reflection in y equals x swaps run

00:02:48.764 --> 00:02:49.263
and rise,

00:02:49.864 --> 00:02:51.784
so the slope at the matching point becomes

00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:52.525
its reciprocal,

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:54.266
one over the old slope.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:55.543
Here's the whole reason.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:57.797
On e to the x, the slope at

00:02:57.858 --> 00:02:59.485
each point equals its height.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:02.534
The reflection turns that height into the x

00:03:02.575 --> 00:03:04.319
coordinate on the natural log curve.

00:03:04.821 --> 00:03:06.729
And it turns the slope into one over

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:07.151
itself.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:10.238
So on the natural log curve, the slope

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:12.463
at x is one over x.

00:03:13.306 --> 00:03:15.172
At height four, e to the x has

00:03:15.253 --> 00:03:15.814
slope four.

00:03:16.558 --> 00:03:18.848
After the reflection, x is four, and the

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:19.934
slope is a quarter.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:23.043
At height a half, the slope a half

00:03:23.124 --> 00:03:25.629
becomes two, at x equals a half.

00:03:26.353 --> 00:03:29.248
That's why gradient times x is always one.

00:03:30.236 --> 00:03:31.469
The app says the same thing.

00:03:32.054 --> 00:03:34.818
With four thousand users, new users arrive at

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.820
four thousand a week,

00:03:36.362 --> 00:03:38.345
so the next thousand takes about a quarter

00:03:38.386 --> 00:03:38.725
of a week.

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:41.448
Now the proof the syllabus asks for, with

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:42.071
the chain rule.

00:03:42.913 --> 00:03:44.055
For every positive x,

00:03:44.737 --> 00:03:46.942
e to the natural log of x equals

00:03:47.163 --> 00:03:47.283
x,

00:03:47.784 --> 00:03:49.528
because the natural log is the power you

00:03:49.589 --> 00:03:51.252
raise e to, to get x.

00:03:52.115 --> 00:03:53.303
Differentiate both sides.

00:03:54.068 --> 00:03:54.551
On the right,

00:03:55.095 --> 00:03:56.223
x has derivative one.

00:03:56.948 --> 00:03:59.212
On the left, the power is an inside

00:03:59.294 --> 00:04:00.796
part, the natural log of x.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.785
The chain rule says copy e to that

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:07.055
power, then multiply by the inside part's derivative,

00:04:07.336 --> 00:04:08.539
the very gradient we're after.

00:04:09.262 --> 00:04:09.442
So e

00:04:09.986 --> 00:04:11.974
to the natural log of x, times the

00:04:11.995 --> 00:04:13.139
gradient, is one.

00:04:13.963 --> 00:04:15.388
But e to the natural log of x

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:15.849
is just x.

00:04:16.894 --> 00:04:19.164
So x times the gradient is one,

00:04:19.848 --> 00:04:21.213
the pattern our readout showed.

00:04:22.217 --> 00:04:24.403
Divide by x, which is fine because x

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:24.924
is positive.

00:04:25.527 --> 00:04:27.397
The derivative of the natural log of x

00:04:27.821 --> 00:04:28.665
is one over x.

00:04:29.309 --> 00:04:30.812
Not one over the natural log of x,

00:04:31.154 --> 00:04:33.218
which doesn't even exist at x equals one.

00:04:33.998 --> 00:04:35.110
Notice where this lives.

00:04:35.735 --> 00:04:37.836
The natural log of x only exists for

00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:40.639
positive x, because e to any power is

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.059
positive.

00:04:41.762 --> 00:04:43.726
That's why the reflected curve stays to the

00:04:43.868 --> 00:04:46.234
right of the y axis, and never touches

00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:46.374
it.

00:04:47.077 --> 00:04:49.301
The expression one over x would make sense

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.405
for negative x, but the natural log doesn't

00:04:51.426 --> 00:04:53.429
exist there, so this rule is for x

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:54.812
greater than zero only.

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:56.056
Let's use it.

00:04:56.722 --> 00:04:59.615
Differentiate y equals the natural log of three

00:04:59.756 --> 00:05:01.102
x, for positive x.

00:05:02.007 --> 00:05:03.152
A log law helps first.

00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:05.281
The log of a product is the sum

00:05:05.301 --> 00:05:07.268
of the logs, so this is the natural

00:05:07.308 --> 00:05:09.335
log of three, plus the natural log of

00:05:09.556 --> 00:05:09.677
x.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.047
The natural log of three is just a

00:05:12.168 --> 00:05:14.258
number, so its derivative is zero.

00:05:15.262 --> 00:05:17.006
So d y by d x is one

00:05:17.187 --> 00:05:19.072
over x, just like the natural log of

00:05:19.192 --> 00:05:19.272
x.

00:05:20.239 --> 00:05:20.661
Surprised?

00:05:21.646 --> 00:05:23.348
On the graph, the natural log of three

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.813
x is the natural log curve slid up

00:05:25.955 --> 00:05:27.076
by the natural log of three.

00:05:27.758 --> 00:05:29.645
A slide up keeps every slope at the

00:05:29.686 --> 00:05:30.228
same x.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.518
So three over x is wrong.

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:35.710
Now compare three times the natural log of

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:35.951
x.

00:05:36.553 --> 00:05:38.481
Find its gradient at x equals two.

00:05:39.344 --> 00:05:41.894
Two videos ago, stretching a curve upward by

00:05:42.055 --> 00:05:44.705
three made every slope three times bigger.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:46.846
That's the constant multiple rule.

00:05:47.649 --> 00:05:49.696
So d y by d x is three

00:05:49.856 --> 00:05:50.277
over x.

00:05:51.061 --> 00:05:53.346
At x equals two, that's three over two,

00:05:53.768 --> 00:05:54.408
one point five.

00:05:55.532 --> 00:05:57.898
On the graph, our triangle at x equals

00:05:57.939 --> 00:05:59.984
two had run two and rise one.

00:06:00.686 --> 00:06:03.254
Stretch it upward, and the rise becomes three.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:06.525
So a number inside the log shifts the

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:07.448
curve vertically,

00:06:07.991 --> 00:06:10.479
but a number in front stretches every slope.

00:06:11.156 --> 00:06:12.163
Here's a way to remember it.

00:06:12.628 --> 00:06:14.241
Flip the graph, flip the slope.

00:06:14.988 --> 00:06:16.595
E to the x has slope equal to

00:06:16.637 --> 00:06:17.018
its height.

00:06:17.621 --> 00:06:20.046
Reflect it in y equals x, and that

00:06:20.148 --> 00:06:22.793
height becomes x, while the slope turns into

00:06:22.975 --> 00:06:23.896
one over itself.

00:06:24.559 --> 00:06:26.544
So the natural log of x has slope

00:06:26.746 --> 00:06:28.792
one over x, for positive x.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:31.921
Your turn.

00:06:32.506 --> 00:06:35.232
A curve has equation y equals two times

00:06:35.274 --> 00:06:37.619
the natural log of x, minus three x,

00:06:37.961 --> 00:06:39.043
for positive x.

00:06:39.746 --> 00:06:41.990
Find its gradient at x equals two.

00:06:42.657 --> 00:06:43.468
Pause here to answer.

00:06:54.001 --> 00:06:56.150
Two times the natural log of x has

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.102
derivative two over x, and three x has

00:06:59.142 --> 00:06:59.845
derivative three.

00:07:00.407 --> 00:07:02.698
So d y by d x is two

00:07:02.859 --> 00:07:04.085
over x, minus three.

00:07:04.828 --> 00:07:06.713
At x equals two, two over two is

00:07:06.974 --> 00:07:07.134
one,

00:07:07.716 --> 00:07:09.721
and one minus three is minus two.

00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:12.163
If you wrote one over x for the

00:07:12.183 --> 00:07:14.585
first term, you treated two times the natural

00:07:14.625 --> 00:07:16.626
log of x like the natural log of

00:07:16.746 --> 00:07:17.207
two x.

00:07:17.828 --> 00:07:19.916
The two in front stretches the slope, so

00:07:19.998 --> 00:07:20.378
it stays.

00:07:22.035 --> 00:07:22.425
Last one.

00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:25.391
For f of x equals the natural log

00:07:25.412 --> 00:07:28.275
of x cubed, with x positive, find f

00:07:28.336 --> 00:07:28.976
prime of x.

00:07:29.538 --> 00:07:31.326
Then find the value of x where the

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:32.271
gradient is six.

00:07:32.936 --> 00:07:33.921
Pause here to answer.

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:46.391
Use the log law first.

00:07:46.895 --> 00:07:48.878
For positive x, the natural log of x

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.403
cubed is three times the natural log of

00:07:51.424 --> 00:07:51.463
x,

00:07:52.546 --> 00:07:54.709
so f prime of x is three over

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:55.049
x.

00:07:55.932 --> 00:07:57.819
Set three over x equal to six, and

00:07:58.061 --> 00:07:58.884
x is a half.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:01.281
That's positive, so it's in the domain.

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:03.966
One over x cubed is wrong.

00:08:04.653 --> 00:08:06.667
You can't just write one over the inside.

00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:09.050
Rewrite with the log law first.

00:08:10.741 --> 00:08:12.926
So the natural log of x is e

00:08:12.966 --> 00:08:15.351
to the x reflected in y equals x,

00:08:15.832 --> 00:08:17.696
and its slope is one over x, for

00:08:17.737 --> 00:08:18.358
positive x.

00:08:19.142 --> 00:08:21.680
Next: what about log base two of x?

00:08:22.264 --> 00:08:24.342
It's the natural log curve, stretched upward.

00:08:25.027 --> 00:08:25.529
By how much,

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:27.239
and what does that do to its slope?

00:08:29.815 --> 00:08:30.597
Here are the key notes.

00:08:31.181 --> 00:08:32.568
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:32.609 --> 00:08:33.071
to copy them.
